WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.716
Filtering is one of the most important skills in data analysis.

2
00:00:04.716 --> 00:00:09.700
In this lesson, you will learn to keep only the rows you need with

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.820
filter, and you will see a very common mistake and how R kindly tells you the fix. Let's filter together.

4
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.131
I write a note:

5
00:00:20.131 --> 00:00:21.562
filtering a data frame.

6
00:00:21.562 --> 00:00:25.140
Our goal is a table with only male household heads.

7
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.015
Male Only Demographic, arrow, demographic, pipe, filter.

8
00:00:32.015 --> 00:00:34.195
Inside filter, the condition:

9
00:00:34.195 --> 00:00:36.920
Gender equals Male, in quotes.

10
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:48.963
Run it.

11
00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:50.870
And here is an error.

12
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.067
But read it slowly, because R is helping us. It says:

13
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:56.974
we detected a named input.

14
00:00:56.974 --> 00:01:00.789
This usually means you used equals instead of double equals.

15
00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:03.460
Did you mean Gender, double equals, Male?

16
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.988
Remember our comparison lesson?

17
00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:16.776
One equals sign assigns.

18
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.563
Two equals signs compare.

19
00:01:18.563 --> 00:01:23.480
Inside filter, we are asking a question, so we need two.

20
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.897
I add the second equals sign, and run it again. It works!

21
00:01:47.897 --> 00:01:50.637
A new table appears in the Environment:

22
00:01:50.637 --> 00:01:52.594
one hundred thirty three rows.

23
00:01:52.594 --> 00:01:54.160
Only male household heads.

24
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.880
Let's open it with View, and check the Gender column.

25
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.720
Every row says Male. Perfect.

26
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.100
Now the same for women.

27
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:36.180
I copy the line, change the name to Female Only Demographic, and the value to Female.

28
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:43.985
Run it.

29
00:02:43.985 --> 00:02:45.947
This table has two rows.

30
00:02:45.947 --> 00:02:49.872
So in this sample, two households are headed by women.

31
00:02:49.872 --> 00:02:52.620
Filtering helps us find these answers quickly.

32
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.840
Next, I write a note: complex filter.

33
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:11.120
In the next lesson, we will filter with several conditions at once. Well done!

